INT. LEVINE & SON PAWN - DAY

A narrow shop crammed with guitars, wristwatches, stuffed marlin, a rack of wedding rings behind glass. A bell over the door. Dusty sun through barred windows.

ABRAHAM LEVINE, 70, pawnbroker, green visor, rolled sleeves, polishes a trumpet. His son JOSHUA, 40s, in a suit that doesn't fit, tallies receipts at the end of the counter.

The bell rings. A WOMAN, 30s, enters. Raincoat, wet hair, a small velvet pouch gripped in both hands. She stands just inside the door as though the floor might give way.

ABRAHAM
Good afternoon.

WOMAN
Is it?

ABRAHAM
For some of us.

She crosses to the counter. Sets the pouch down. Pulls the drawstring. A gold locket slides out. Joshua stops tallying.

JOSHUA
Dad.

ABRAHAM
I see it.

WOMAN
It was my mother's.

ABRAHAM
It's beautiful.

JOSHUA
It's worth four hundred, easy.

WOMAN
Eight.

JOSHUA
Four.

WOMAN
It has a picture inside.

ABRAHAM
May I?

She nods. Abraham opens it with a thumbnail. Stares. Closes it.

ABRAHAM (CONT'D)
Joshua. Look at this.

JOSHUA
What?

ABRAHAM
Just look.

Joshua takes the locket, opens it. His face changes.

JOSHUA
Dad. That's...

ABRAHAM
Rosalie Pinsky.

WOMAN
I'm Rosalie.

ABRAHAM
No. The locket belonged to...

ROSALIE
My mother. Hannah Pinsky. She pawned it here in 1991.

ABRAHAM
She did. And she never came back.

ROSALIE
She died.

JOSHUA
We're sorry.

ROSALIE
Don't be. I'm here to buy it back.

ABRAHAM
It's yours.

JOSHUA
Dad, it's inventory.

ABRAHAM
Joshua, a locket this old isn't inventory. It's a promise.

JOSHUA
We have a business.

ABRAHAM
We have a mother.

Joshua looks at the locket. At Rosalie. At his father. He closes the drawer.

JOSHUA
Take it.

ROSALIE
I'll pay for it.

ABRAHAM
You already did.

Rosalie closes her fingers around the locket. The bell above the door rings softly as a draft touches it.
